{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"adf1a94eccf778cb","name":"card_unit_defiant_mk_i","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/units/card_unit_defiant_mk_i","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"DEFIANT Mk I","text":"Has +2 attack on your turn if you deployed a British infantry last turn.","flavorText":"The Boulton Paul Defiant Mk I was a unique British \"turret fighter\" introduced in 1939. Designed to combat unescorted bombers, it was notable for lacking forward-firing guns. Instead, it relied on a powered dorsal turret located behind the pilot, armed with four .303 Browning machine guns. This concept proved effective briefly during the Dunkirk evacuation, as Luftwaffe pilots often mistook the aircraft for a Hawker Hurricane and attacked from the rear, flying directly into the gunner's sights. However, once the Germans adapted their tactics, the Defiant suffered heavy losses in daylight operations. Outclassed by faster, more agile enemy fighters, the Mk I was withdrawn from day combat and reassigned to night fighting. Equipped with airborne interception radar, the Defiant found a new niche role, achieving moderate success during the Blitz, between September 1940 and May 1941, before being superseded by faster twin-engine aircraft like the Beaufighter and Mosquito. The Mk I remained in service for training, air-sea rescue, and electronic warfare roles. Over 700 Mk I units were produced before the improved Mk II entered service. Though short-lived as a frontline fighter, the Defiant filled a critical gap during a period of RAF vulnerability and remains notable as one of the few operational turret fighters of WWII.","faction":"Britain","type":"fighter","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":2,"operationCost":1,"attack":2,"defense":2},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.bond"],"Name":"card_unit_defiant_mk_i","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_defiant_mk_i_title","SourceString":"DEFIANT Mk I","LocalizedString":"DEFIANT Mk I"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::fighter","attack":2,"defense":2,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_defiant_mk_i_text","SourceString":"Has +2 attack on your turn if you deployed a British infantry last turn.","LocalizedString":"Has +2 attack on your turn if you deployed a British infantry last turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_defiant_mk_i_history","SourceString":"The Boulton Paul Defiant Mk I was a unique British \"turret fighter\" introduced in 1939. Designed to combat unescorted bombers, it was notable for lacking forward-firing guns. Instead, it relied on a powered dorsal turret located behind the pilot, armed with four .303 Browning machine guns. This concept proved effective briefly during the Dunkirk evacuation, as Luftwaffe pilots often mistook the aircraft for a Hawker Hurricane and attacked from the rear, flying directly into the gunner's sights. However, once the Germans adapted their tactics, the Defiant suffered heavy losses in daylight operations. Outclassed by faster, more agile enemy fighters, the Mk I was withdrawn from day combat and reassigned to night fighting. Equipped with airborne interception radar, the Defiant found a new niche role, achieving moderate success during the Blitz, between September 1940 and May 1941, before being superseded by faster twin-engine aircraft like the Beaufighter and Mosquito. The Mk I remained in service for training, air-sea rescue, and electronic warfare roles. Over 700 Mk I units were produced before the improved Mk II entered service. Though short-lived as a frontline fighter, the Defiant filled a critical gap during a period of RAF vulnerability and remains notable as one of the few operational turret fighters of WWII.","LocalizedString":"The Boulton Paul Defiant Mk I was a unique British \"turret fighter\" introduced in 1939. Designed to combat unescorted bombers, it was notable for lacking forward-firing guns. Instead, it relied on a powered dorsal turret located behind the pilot, armed with four .303 Browning machine guns. This concept proved effective briefly during the Dunkirk evacuation, as Luftwaffe pilots often mistook the aircraft for a Hawker Hurricane and attacked from the rear, flying directly into the gunner's sights. However, once the Germans adapted their tactics, the Defiant suffered heavy losses in daylight operations. Outclassed by faster, more agile enemy fighters, the Mk I was withdrawn from day combat and reassigned to night fighting. Equipped with airborne interception radar, the Defiant found a new niche role, achieving moderate success during the Blitz, between September 1940 and May 1941, before being superseded by faster twin-engine aircraft like the Beaufighter and Mosquito. The Mk I remained in service for training, air-sea rescue, and electronic warfare roles. Over 700 Mk I units were produced before the improved Mk II entered service. Though short-lived as a frontline fighter, the Defiant filled a critical gap during a period of RAF vulnerability and remains notable as one of the few operational turret fighters of WWII."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Passive","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/DEFIANT_Mk_I.DEFIANT_Mk_I","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":343.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_defiant_mk_i_photo","SourceString":"A Defiant Mk I of No. 264 Squadron in 1940.","LocalizedString":"A Defiant Mk I of No. 264 Squadron in 1940."},"blowUpExplosion":{"ObjectName":"ParticleSystem'P_ExplosionAircraftM'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Explosions/Medium/Aircraft/P_ExplosionAircraftM.193"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeMedium_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/C